 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "e1",
   "source_id": "A.6.1",
   "source_number": "A.6.1",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "eq-cofactor-row",
   "prompt": "<p>Compute the determinant of the following matrices:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>$\\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\end{bmatrix}$</li><li>$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$</li><li>$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$</li><li>$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\end{bmatrix}$</li><li>$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$</li><li>$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 7 \\end{bmatrix}$</li><li>$\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$</li><li>$\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$</li></ol>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 3
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": -5
     },
     {
      "part": "c",
      "value": 0
     },
     {
      "part": "d",
      "value": 24
     },
     {
      "part": "e",
      "value": 12
     },
     {
      "part": "f",
      "value": 85
     },
     {
      "part": "g",
      "value": 84
     },
     {
      "part": "h",
      "value": -3
     }
    ],
    "solution": "a) The determinant of a $1 \\times 1$ matrix is its only entry: $3$. b) $1 \\cdot 1 - 3 \\cdot 2 = -5$. c) $2 \\cdot 2 - 1 \\cdot 4 = 0$. d) The matrix is upper triangular, so its determinant is the product of the diagonal entries: $1 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 = 24$. e) Expand along the third row, whose only nonzero entry is $a_{32} = 2$, with sign ${(-1)}^{3+2} = -1$: $-2 \\det \\left( \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right] \\right) = -2(-6) = 12$. f) Expand along the first row: $2 (6 \\cdot 7 - 3 \\cdot 9) - 1 (8 \\cdot 7 - 3 \\cdot 7) + 3 (8 \\cdot 9 - 6 \\cdot 7) = 2 \\cdot 15 - 35 + 3 \\cdot 30 = 85$. g) Expand along the first column, whose only nonzero entry is $a_{31} = 3$, with sign ${(-1)}^{3+1} = 1$: $3 \\det \\left( \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right] \\right)$, and expanding that along its first column gives $3 \\cdot 2 \\, (2 \\cdot 4 - (-3) \\cdot 2) = 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 14 = 84$. h) Expand along the first row, where only $a_{12} = 1$ and $a_{13} = 2$ are nonzero: $-1 \\cdot \\det \\left( \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right] \\right) + 2 \\det \\left( \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right] \\right) = -1 \\cdot (-3) + 2 \\cdot (-3) = -3$, each $3 \\times 3$ determinant by its own first row: $1 \\cdot 8 + 1 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot (-6) = -3$ and $1 \\cdot 4 - 1 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot (-3) = -3$."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e2",
   "source_id": "A.6.2",
   "source_number": "A.6.2",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "thm-a-6-2",
   "prompt": "<p>For which $x$ are the following matrices singular (not invertible).</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & x \\end{bmatrix}$</li><li>$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & x \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$</li><li>$\\begin{bmatrix} x & 1 \\\\ 4 & x \\end{bmatrix}$</li><li>$\\begin{bmatrix} x & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$</li></ol>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 3
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": 4
     },
     {
      "part": "c, x > 0",
      "value": 2
     },
     {
      "part": "c, x < 0",
      "value": -2
     },
     {
      "part": "d",
      "value": 0.5
     }
    ],
    "solution": "By Theorem A.6.2 a matrix is singular exactly when its determinant is zero. a) $2x - 3 \\cdot 2 = 2x - 6 = 0$, so $x = 3$. b) $2 \\cdot 2 - x \\cdot 1 = 4 - x = 0$, so $x = 4$. c) $x^2 - 4 = 0$, so $x = 2$ or $x = -2$. d) Expand along the first row: $x (4 \\cdot 2 - 2 \\cdot 6) - 0 + 1 (1 \\cdot 6 - 4 \\cdot 1) = -4x + 2 = 0$, so $x = \\frac{1}{2}$."
   }
  },
