   "cite": "thm-a-6-1",
   "prompt": "<p>Suppose</p>$$ L = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & \\pi & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2^8 & 5 & -99 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad U = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 9 & 1 & -\\sin(1) \\\\ 0 & 1 & 88 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} . $$<p>Let $\\kA = LU$. Compute $\\det(\\kA)$ in a simple way, without computing what is $\\kA$. Hint: First read off $\\det(L)$ and $\\det(U)$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "number",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "value": 5,
    "solution": "$L$ is lower triangular with every diagonal entry $1$, so $\\det(L) = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 = 1$; $U$ is upper triangular with diagonal entries $5, 1, 1, 1$, so $\\det(U) = 5$. By Theorem A.6.1, $\\det(\\kA) = \\det(LU) = \\det(L)\\det(U) = 1 \\cdot 5 = 5$."
   }
