   "cite": "area-scale",
   "prompt": "<p>Consider the linear mapping from ${\\mathbb R}^2$ to ${\\mathbb R}^2$ given by the matrix $\\kA = \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & x \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]$ for some number $x$. You wish to make $\\kA$ such that it doubles the area of every geometric figure. What are the possibilities for $x$ (there are two answers).</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "x with det(A) = 2",
      "value": -0.5
     },
     {
      "part": "x with det(A) = −2",
      "value": 1.5
     }
    ],
    "solution": "$\\kA$ multiplies every area by $\\lvert\\det(\\kA)\\rvert$, so it doubles areas exactly when $\\lvert\\det(\\kA)\\rvert = 2$. Here $\\det(\\kA) = 1 \\cdot 1 - x \\cdot 2 = 1 - 2x$. Then $1 - 2x = 2$ gives $x = -\\frac{1}{2}$ (orientation kept), and $1 - 2x = -2$ gives $x = \\frac{3}{2}$ (the axes flipped)."
   }
